.TH CHMAP_INSERT_ELEM 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_insert_elem \- insert a key-value pair into the hash map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

ccol_retval_t chmap_insert_elem(chmap chm,
                                 const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                 const cmap_pair *val_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Inserts a key-value pair into the hash map. If the key is already there,
the new value replaces the old one. When the load factor crosses a
threshold, the map resizes.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm        Hash map to insert into
key_pair   Key to insert (copied into map)
val_pair   Value to associate with key (copied into map)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success               - A new key was inserted
ccol_key_already_present   - The key was already there and its value was
                             updated
ccol_invalid_args          - chm, key_pair, or val_pair is NULL or size is 0;
                             also returned when key_pair->size does not
                             exactly match the byte size of the key type,
                             for any fixed-width numeric key type (char,
                             short, int, long, long long, their unsigned
                             counterparts, float, double, long double, or
                             a pointer type), regardless of which backend
                             the map uses; also returned when
                             val_pair->size does not exactly match the byte
                             size of the value type the map was created
                             with, for any fixed-width value type, and
                             regardless of which backend the map uses. A
                             value type with no fixed width (ccol_string
                             and ccol_other_types) is never checked,
                             because every entry of such a map
                             legitimately carries its own size
ccol_container_full        - Map has reached ccol_max_elem_count
ccol_not_enough_memory     - Memory allocation failed
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
key_pair->ptr needs no particular alignment, because the map hashes and
compares the key bytes through memcpy, never through a pointer cast to the
key type. So it can point straight at key bytes at any offset in a larger
buffer, such as a field of a packed network frame or a memory-mapped
record, and you don't have to copy them into an aligned local variable
first
Average O(1) complexity
The map copies the key data and the value data into itself
When the key is already there, the map frees the old value and replaces
it
key_pair and val_pair may point into the map itself. Each can be the
pair that chmap_get_elem_ref(3) filled for any key of the same map, or
any other pair that points at the bytes of a stored key or value. The
map reads both pairs before it changes anything, so this also holds for
an insert that resizes the map and frees the storage they point at.
Once the insert returns, the rule of chmap_get_elem_ref(3) applies to
such a pair, and the insert has ended the validity of its ptr
The map resizes itself when the load factor goes above its threshold.
With open addressing, an insert of a new key doubles the table once the
live entries fill more than 0.70 of it; a delete leaves no marker for a
deleted slot, so only live entries count
A value of the wrong width is rejected at the INSERT and not at a later
read. Without that check the insert would report success, and the
value-size guard of chmap_get(3) would stop the process on the first
read of that key, at a call site that did nothing wrong
The type-inferred chmap_insert(3) macro never hits that rejection,
because it first converts the value through the map's declared value
type, so every pair it builds has exactly the right width
For a float or double key, chmap_construct(3) gives the key equality
contract (bitwise, except that -0.0 becomes 0.0)
For a long double key, chmap_construct(3) gives the key equality contract
(by value, unlike every other key type)
A map created with a custom key equality procedure compares keys with
it; see chmap_create_full(3)
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_insert (3),
.BR chmap_delete_elem (3),
.BR chmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3)
